\[
(75 + 117i)z + \frac{96 + 144i}{z} = (75 + 117i) \cdot 4e^{i\theta} + \frac{96 + 144i}{4e^{i\theta}}
\]

Simplifying each term, we have:

\[
(75 + 117i) \cdot 4e^{i\theta} = 300e^{i\theta} + 468ie^{i\theta}
\]
\[
\frac{96 + 144i}{4e^{i\theta}} = 24e^{-i\theta} + 36ie^{-i\theta}
\]

Combining these, the expression becomes:

\[
300e^{i\theta} + 468ie^{i\theta} + 24e^{-i\theta} + 36ie^{-i\theta}
\]

Separating the real and imaginary parts, we use Euler's formula \(e^{i\theta} = \cos\theta + i\sin\theta\) and \(e^{-i\theta} = \cos\theta - i\sin\theta\):

\[
\text{Real part: } 300\cos\theta + 468\sin\theta + 24\cos\theta - 36\sin\theta = 324\cos\theta + 432\sin\theta
\]
\[
\text{Imaginary part: } 300\sin\theta - 468\cos\theta - 24\sin\theta + 36\cos\theta = 276\sin\theta - 432\cos\theta
\]

The real part of the expression is \(324\cos\theta + 432\sin\theta\). To find the maximum value of this expression, we recognize it as a linear combination of sine and cosine functions. The maximum value of \(A\cos\theta + B\sin\theta\) is \(\sqrt{A^2 + B^2}\). Here, \(A = 324\) and \(B = 432\):

\[
